  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES satellite imagery reveals cloudy skies overhead \nthe Upper Great Lakes underneath the exit region of a departing \nupper jet and weak embedded mid-level shortwave. These features are \ntouching off light rain showers across the western UP. Nothing more \nthan like rain or sprinkles are expected considering the stout \nmidlevel and surface dry layer hydrometeors have to fight through to \nreach the surface. Synoptic support must be adequate enough \nconsidering several ASOS stations are reporting light rain in \nnorthern WI despite sfc Td depressions 15-20F. Regardless, expecting \nthese showers to graze the UP through morning before getting shunted \nsouth by incoming ridging and building surface high pressure. \nNortherly winds ahead of the aforementioned building sfc high will \nkeep temperatures near the climatological normal of 40s to low 50s \nfor the first time in a while today. Seasonable temps will last for \none whole day before being raised above normal again tonight as a \nshortwave lifts a weak surface low west of Lake Superior. Isentropic \nascent ahead of this wave will force an area of light rain in the \nwestern UP through Tuesday while lifting temps back into the 50s. \nAnother more vigorous shortwave lifts into Ontario Wednesday, \nfurther pushing temps back into the 60s to near 70. This drags a \ncold front through the UP Wednesday night through Thursday morning, \nkicking off better chances for showers. Deterministic models and \ntheir ensemble counterparts generally agree on quiet weather \ndominating through much of the weekend, potentially keeping \nHalloween night dry, but diverge towards the end of the month as a \nmore zonal/SW flow aloft pattern takes shape, sending shortwave \nactivity back into the region. At this time, ensembles don't suggest \nany widespread meaningful rain across the area with 24hr prob of QPF \n>0.1\" only reaching 60-80% on any given day for portions of the UP. \nAdditionally, above normal temperatures dominate through the rest of \nthe month into early November with seemingly no end in sight as the \nCPC extended probabilities favor above normal temps through the 1-3 \nweek and 3-4 week outlooks. All global ensembles highlight strong \npositive 850mb temp anomalies even into their extended 200+ hour \nrange.",
